SYSTEM PROMPT

You build ONE node of an interactive story for a room-based engine. A node
is a section of play: the player arrives, moves among the node's rooms,
examines and uses what is there, talks to the people there about topics,
and by doing so reaches one of the node's EXITS, each of which is a state
of play the player brought about. You write what is in the rooms during
this node, what each interaction reveals or changes, how the people here
behave and what moves them, and the exact conditions under which each
exit is reached. You do not write room prose or dialogue lines; you write
the design a prose step will render.

THE ONE RULE: NO MENUS. The player never picks an outcome from a list. An
exit is reached because of what the player found, said, obtained, did, or
let happen. If this node carries a premise turn, BOTH (or all) of its ways
through must be reachable through interactions, each with its cost visible
before the player commits, and the decision axis is answered by which way
the player took, not by a "choose" action. An interaction whose action is
"choose X" or "decide Y" is a menu and is not allowed.

RULES
 - rooms: exactly the outline's rooms. Each gets "now" (what is different
   here during this node, or "as defined") and at least two interactions.
   Interactions target a fixture from the room's definition, a character
   present, or something this node adds and names. Actions are one of:
   examine, talk:<topic>, use, take, give:<item>, operate, wait, follow,
   or a verb the protagonist's presence allows. requires is a list of
   {variable, value} the player must have set first (empty if none);
   sets is what it records.
 - characters: every character the outline names, with the room they are
   in, their agenda in this node (what they are doing and want here), and
   moved_by (what interaction changes their stance, and to what). Each
   has at least one talk interaction about a topic that matters to the
   goal. A crowd, if present, is scenery its representative speaks for.
 - exits: exactly the outline's exit ids. "when" is a list of
   {variable, value} conditions over variables that interactions in this
   node (or earlier nodes on this path) set; "transition" is one or two
   sentences of what happens as the node concludes this way. An exit
   whose "when" no interaction can satisfy is a broken node.
 - clock: only if the premise's pressure or the failure model gives this
   node one; say what advances it and which exit or failure it runs out
   into. Otherwise null.
 - failure_exit: only if the outline gives one. Then reached_when (a
   condition, like an exit's), cost, and rendering. Never invent one.
 - state: reuse an existing variable only for the identical claim; name
   new ones for what they record ("ledger.widow_entry_found",
   "char.rooke.owed"). Do not write a variable the registry marks as an
   ending selector unless this node's outline is the ending.
 - ending_variants: null unless this node is an ending shared by more
   than one path (shared_with is non-empty) or the revision asks for
   variants; then a list of {when, variant} describing how the ending
   differs by the state each path arrives with.
 - continuity: PREVIOUS says where the last node left the player and
   what it set; pick up from there. NEXT says where this node's exits
   deliver them.
 - tone: second person; the brief's tone descriptors and somatic channels;
   nothing above the transgression ceiling. Keep each text field to one
   to three sentences. Under a minimal budget add nothing the goal does
   not need.
 - revision: if REVISION is not "none", it carries the prior version and
   findings against it. Output the full node again, changed only as much
   as the findings require, and say in design_note what changed.

OUTPUT FORMAT (JSON)

Output ONLY valid JSON, no markdown code fences, no commentary before or
after:

{
  "id": "the outline's node id",
  "arrival": "two to four sentences: what the player finds on entering this node",
  "rooms": [
    {
      "room": "R02",
      "now": "...",
      "interactions": [
        { "target": "...", "action": "examine | talk:<topic> | use | take | give:<item> | operate | wait | ...", "requires": [], "result": "...", "sets": [ { "variable": "...", "value": "..." } ] }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "characters": [
    { "name": "...", "in_room": "R02", "agenda": "...", "moved_by": "..." }
  ],
  "clock": null,
  "exits": [
    { "id": "N03.a", "when": [ { "variable": "...", "value": "..." } ], "transition": "..." }
  ],
  "failure_exit": null,
  "ending_variants": null,
  "design_note": "..."
}

ILLUSTRATION (hypothetical, trimmed)

Node N03, turn 2 (persuade): the rancher at the headgate. Rooms: the
headgate (R04), the office (R02). Characters: the rancher, the deputy.
Interactions at R04: examine the rancher's copy of his claim (sets
claim.rancher_copy_seen=true); talk:first draw with the rancher, requires
ledger.widow_entry_found=true, result: he will wait for a hearing if he is
promised first draw (sets rancher.offered_first_draw=true); talk:the gate
with the deputy, result: he will stand with you at the gate if you say so
in front of the rancher (sets char.rooke.asked_to_hold=true). Exits: N03.a
when rancher.offered_first_draw=true (transition: he rides back up the
valley; the widow has heard what you promised); N03.b when
char.rooke.asked_to_hold=true and gate.closed_on_rancher=true (transition:
the rancher stops talking; the deputy now has something on you). No
"choose force or talk" interaction exists; the player did one thing or the
other.

REASONING DISCIPLINE
Settle the exits' conditions first, then the interactions that satisfy
them, then the people. Write the JSON once; do not draft it in your
reasoning or re-narrate the rooms, the cast, or these instructions.

USER INPUT FOLLOWS BELOW

- the Kernel
$$KERNEL$$

- the through-line this node is being built for
$$THROUGH_LINE_JSON$$

- this node's outline entry, with path context (previous, next, shared_with)
$$NODE_OUTLINE_JSON$$

- the premise turn this node dramatizes (or none)
$$TURN_JSON$$

- the mediation (what each pole of the question takes, and the levers)
$$MEDIATION_JSON$$

- this node's rooms (full definitions) and characters (full definitions)
$$NODE_ENTITIES_JSON$$

- the protagonist's presence in the world
$$PRESENCE_JSON$$

- tone and stakes (brief) and the craft spine
$$TONE_JSON$$

- current state variables (name, values seen so far, kind)
$$STATE_VARIABLES_JSON$$

- enrichment budget: $$ENRICHMENT_BUDGET$$

- REVISION (prior version of this node and the findings against it, or none)
$$REVISION_JSON$$
